        {"kind": "h1", "text": "2. Overview & Summary"},
        {"kind": "h2", "text": "2.1 Problem Statement"},
        {"kind": "para", "text": "HaorGrix operations span six third-party platforms — Brevo, cPanel, Google Analytics 4, Google Search Console, Meta Ads, and WordPress with WooCommerce. Routine work on these platforms (pulling a performance report, purging a cache after a deploy, checking whether a page is indexed, pausing an over-spending ad set) is performed by hand through six separate web UIs. That work is slow, it is not reproducible, it leaves no audit trail, and it cannot be delegated to an AI agent because no machine-readable interface exists that an agent can safely drive."},
        {"kind": "para", "text": "Each platform does expose an API, but each has a different authentication model, pagination scheme, rate-limit behaviour, and error vocabulary. Writing throwaway scripts against them — which is what happened before this project — produces code that is neither shared, reviewed, nor guarded against destructive mistakes."},
        {"kind": "h2", "text": "2.2 Proposed Solution"},
        {"kind": "para", "text": "A monorepo of six Model Context Protocol servers, one per platform, each exposing that platform's capabilities as typed MCP tools over stdio. An AI agent mounts the servers it needs and drives them through a single uniform protocol, while each server owns the platform-specific concerns — auth, pagination, retries, rate limiting, and guard rails on destructive operations — behind a stable tool surface."},
        {"kind": "para", "text": "The six servers expose 161 tools in total. They are consolidated into one repository so that credential handling, gitignore discipline, tool-naming conventions, and release process are set once and applied uniformly, rather than drifting per project."},
        {"kind": "h2", "text": "2.3 Background & Context"},
        {"kind": "para", "text": "The servers were built incrementally between roughly 2026-06 and 2026-07 as individual needs arose, initially as separate working directories. They were first consolidated into a single local git repository on 2026-07-07, extended with the Google Analytics server on 2026-07-15, and the Meta Ads server was rewritten to v2 on 2026-07-21 to add pagination, complete insights coverage, and guarded writes."},
        {"kind": "para", "text": "On 2026-07-22 the repository was audited before being published to GitHub. That audit found two Google service-account private keys committed in the git history. The history was rewritten to purge them and the repository was pushed private. Both keys must be treated as compromised until rotated; this is tracked as an open action in section 13."},
        {"kind": "h2", "text": "2.4 Strategic Fit"},
        {"kind": "para", "text": "This portfolio is the execution layer for HaorGrix's agent-driven operations. Client-facing work — SEO campaigns, WordPress site management, email marketing, paid media — is delivered on these six platforms. Every tool added here converts a manual, unlogged, per-client task into a repeatable operation an agent can perform, which is what makes the service model scale beyond the number of hours the team can bill."},
        {"kind": "h2", "text": "2.5 Objectives & Business Value"},
        {"kind": "bullets", "items": [
            "Make all six operational platforms reachable by AI agents through one uniform protocol.",
            "Cut the manual effort of routine platform operations (reporting, cache purges, indexing checks, campaign hygiene) by making them single tool calls.",
            "Prevent agent-driven accidents on production systems and live ad spend through explicit write guards, confirmation gates, and audit logging.",
            "Establish one credential and release discipline across all servers instead of six divergent ad-hoc setups.",
        ]},

